### What are the core entities?

The core entities are `Hotel`, `Room`, `RoomType`, `Reservation`, `Guest`, and `CancellationPolicy`.

```java
package com.bank.hotel.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public enum RoomType {
    STANDARD(new BigDecimal("3000")),
    DELUXE(new BigDecimal("5000")),
    SUITE(new BigDecimal("10000"));

    private final BigDecimal nightlyRate;

    RoomType(BigDecimal nightlyRate) {
        this.nightlyRate = nightlyRate;
    }

    public BigDecimal getNightlyRate() { return nightlyRate; }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.hotel.model;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Room {
    private final String roomNumber;
    private final RoomType type;
    private final List<Reservation> reservations;

    public Room(String roomNumber, RoomType type) {
        this.roomNumber = roomNumber;
        this.type = type;
        this.reservations = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public boolean isAvailable(LocalDate checkIn, LocalDate checkOut) {
        return reservations.stream()
                .filter(r -> r.getStatus() != ReservationStatus.CANCELLED)
                .noneMatch(r -> r.overlaps(checkIn, checkOut));
    }

    public void addReservation(Reservation reservation) {
        reservations.add(reservation);
    }

    public String getRoomNumber() { return roomNumber; }
    public RoomType getType() { return type; }
}
```

### How does date-range availability work?

This is the central algorithmic challenge. Two date ranges overlap when the start of one is before the end of the other and vice versa.

```java
package com.bank.hotel.model;

import java.time.LocalDate;

public class Reservation {
    private final String reservationId;
    private final Guest guest;
    private final Room room;
    private final LocalDate checkIn;
    private final LocalDate checkOut;
    private ReservationStatus status;

    public Reservation(String reservationId, Guest guest, Room room,
                       LocalDate checkIn, LocalDate checkOut) {
        this.reservationId = reservationId;
        this.guest = guest;
        this.room = room;
        this.checkIn = checkIn;
        this.checkOut = checkOut;
        this.status = ReservationStatus.CONFIRMED;
    }

    public boolean overlaps(LocalDate otherCheckIn,
                            LocalDate otherCheckOut) {
        return checkIn.isBefore(otherCheckOut)
                && otherCheckIn.isBefore(checkOut);
    }

    public ReservationStatus getStatus() { return status; }
    public void setStatus(ReservationStatus status) {
        this.status = status;
    }
    public LocalDate getCheckIn() { return checkIn; }
    public LocalDate getCheckOut() { return checkOut; }
    public String getReservationId() { return reservationId; }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.hotel.model;

public enum ReservationStatus {
    CONFIRMED, CHECKED_IN, COMPLETED, CANCELLED
}
```

### How does search work?

Search filters by location, date range, room type, and price, returning available rooms.

```java
package com.bank.hotel.service;

import com.bank.hotel.model.*;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class HotelService {
    private final Hotel hotel;

    public HotelService(Hotel hotel) {
        this.hotel = hotel;
    }

    public List<Room> searchAvailable(RoomType type,
                                       LocalDate checkIn,
                                       LocalDate checkOut) {
        return hotel.getRooms().stream()
                .filter(r -> r.getType() == type)
                .filter(r -> r.isAvailable(checkIn, checkOut))
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

### How does booking work?

Booking creates a Reservation in CONFIRMED status. Check-in moves it to CHECKED_IN. Check-out to COMPLETED.

```java
public Reservation book(Guest guest, Room room,
                        LocalDate checkIn, LocalDate checkOut) {
    synchronized (room) {
        if (!room.isAvailable(checkIn, checkOut)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                    "Room " + room.getRoomNumber()
                    + " not available for these dates");
        }
        Reservation res = new Reservation(
                java.util.UUID.randomUUID().toString(),
                guest, room, checkIn, checkOut);
        room.addReservation(res);
        System.out.println("Booked room " + room.getRoomNumber()
                + " for " + guest.getName()
                + " from " + checkIn + " to " + checkOut);
        return res;
    }
}
```

### How does the cancellation policy work?

Cancellation runs through a policy interface so the business can choose different penalty rules.

```java
package com.bank.hotel.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;

public interface CancellationPolicy {
    BigDecimal computePenalty(LocalDate checkIn,
                              LocalDate cancellationDate,
                              BigDecimal totalCost);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.hotel.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit;

public class FreeUntil24hPolicy implements CancellationPolicy {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal computePenalty(LocalDate checkIn,
                                     LocalDate cancellationDate,
                                     BigDecimal totalCost) {
        long daysUntilCheckIn = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(
                cancellationDate, checkIn);
        if (daysUntilCheckIn >= 1) {
            return BigDecimal.ZERO; // free cancellation
        }
        return totalCost; // full charge for late cancel
    }
}
```

### How do you handle concurrency?

Two guests booking the last deluxe room at the same instant must be serialised. The `book` method synchronises on the room object so the availability check and reservation insert happen atomically. In production, this would be a database unique constraint or a row-level lock.

### What about the reservation lifecycle?

A reservation progresses through a clear lifecycle: CONFIRMED when booked, CHECKED_IN on arrival, COMPLETED on departure, or CANCELLED. Invalid transitions are rejected.

```java
public void checkIn(Reservation res) {
    if (res.getStatus() != ReservationStatus.CONFIRMED) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot check in: " + res.getStatus());
    }
    res.setStatus(ReservationStatus.CHECKED_IN);
}

public void checkOut(Reservation res) {
    if (res.getStatus() != ReservationStatus.CHECKED_IN) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot check out: " + res.getStatus());
    }
    res.setStatus(ReservationStatus.COMPLETED);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The overlap condition `a.start < b.end && b.start < a.end` is the foundation. Getting it wrong causes double-booking.
- Cancellation policy as a Strategy lets the business change rules without touching booking logic.
- For high-throughput booking systems, use optimistic locking with version numbers instead of pessimistic synchronisation.
- Pricing can also be a Strategy: flat rate, seasonal surge, loyalty discount.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you prevent double-booking of the same room?
- How would you add seasonal pricing?
- How do you handle overbooking (airline style)?
- How would you scale this to a hotel chain with thousands of properties?
- What is the time complexity of checking availability?
